Here the current primitive is built separately. `openCount` and `closeCount` tell when that primitive is complete. At that moment, the first and last characters are left out before the inner part is added to the result.

### Small walkthrough
For `(())`, the counts become equal after the final `)`. The temporary text is `(())`, so only `()` is copied to the answer.

```java
class Solution {
    public String removeOuterParentheses(String s) {
        StringBuilder answer = new StringBuilder();
        StringBuilder current = new StringBuilder();
        int openCount = 0;
        int closeCount = 0;

        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
            current.append(ch);

            if (ch == '(') {
                openCount++;
            } else {
                closeCount++;
            }

            if (openCount == closeCount) {
                if (current.length() > 2) {
                    answer.append(current, 1, current.length() - 1);
                }
                current.setLength(0);
                openCount = 0;
                closeCount = 0;
            }
        }

        return answer.toString();
    }
}
```

The single pair `()` is an edge case because removing its outside pair leaves nothing. Multiple primitive groups are reset independently.

Time complexity: `O(n)` with a `StringBuilder`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current group and the output.

A depth counter is enough here. When an opening parenthesis appears at depth `0`, it starts a primitive group, so it is skipped. For a closing parenthesis, decrease the depth first. If the new depth is `0`, that closing parenthesis is also skipped.

### Dry run
For `(()())(())`, the primitive groups are `(()())` and `(())`. Removing their outside pairs gives `()()` and `()`, so the result is `()()()`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string removeOuterParentheses(string s) {
        string answer;
        int depth = 0;

        for (char ch : s) {
            if (ch == '(') {
                if (depth > 0) {
                    answer += ch;
                }
                depth++;
            } else {
                depth--;
                if (depth > 0) {
                    answer += ch;
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

The smallest input `()` returns an empty string. The input string is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned string and `O(1)` extra working space.

Instead of adding characters one by one, this code finds where each primitive part ends. The balance returns to zero at that point. The slice between `start + 1` and `end` is the primitive without its first and last parentheses.

For example, `(()())(())` is split at balance zero into `(()())` and `(())`. The slices are `()()` and `()`. Empty primitives such as `()` naturally add an empty slice.

```python
class Solution:
    def removeOuterParentheses(self, s: str) -> str:
        parts = []
        balance = 0
        start = 0

        for end, ch in enumerate(s):
            if ch == '(':
                balance += 1
            else:
                balance -= 1

            if balance == 0:
                parts.append(s[start + 1:end])
                start = end + 1

        return ''.join(parts)
```

An input containing only `()` returns `""`. The original string stays unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the collected slices and final result.

This version uses an integer array as a small stack. The first `(` of a primitive group is pushed but not added. On `)`, pop first, then add it only when the stack still has something inside it. That leaves every inner parenthesis in the answer.

The stack is not needed for the problem, but it makes the opening and closing steps easy to see.

```java
class Solution {
    public String removeOuterParentheses(String s) {
        int[] stack = new int[s.length()];
        int size = 0;
        StringBuilder answer = new StringBuilder();

        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (ch == '(') {
                if (size > 0) {
                    answer.append(ch);
                }
                stack[size++] = 1;
            } else {
                size--;
                if (size > 0) {
                    answer.append(ch);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer.toString();
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each character is handled once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and the output builder.

This keeps the primitive groups separate with a recursive helper. The helper reads one complete group, skips its first and last characters, and then continues with the next group. It is a little more code than the counter-only version, but the split between groups is clear.

The recursion depth can reach `O(n)` for a deeply nested input, so this uses more working memory than a simple loop.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    void collect(const string& s, int& index, string& answer) {
        if (index >= static_cast<int>(s.size())) {
            return;
        }

        int start = index;
        int depth = 0;

        do {
            if (s[index] == '(') {
                depth++;
            } else {
                depth--;
            }
            index++;
        } while (depth != 0);

        answer.append(s, start + 1, index - start - 2);
        collect(s, index, answer);
    }

public:
    string removeOuterParentheses(string s) {
        string answer;
        int index = 0;
        collect(s, index, answer);
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every character is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the output and up to `O(n)` call stack space.

